  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Thalamocortical loop\n\nA relay nucleus and a patch of cortex are wired in both directions through a fiber tract, drawn here as a cylinder. Every synapse is local: the connect radius is 600 um and the patch sits 805 um from the nucleus, so no single synapse spans the gap. A signal crosses the tract in hops, and the length of the tract shows up in how long a trip around the loop takes.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": ""
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 170,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "The relay cells use the TC preset. It rests at -64.4 mV and fires steadily from a current of about 0.66, a sixth of what an RS cell needs, so the noise and input in the loop are enough to keep it going. The published model pairs the preset with a holding current that keeps the cell below threshold until something drives it, which is where a rebound burst comes from.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "thalamic relay"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
